/bg kill and /quit now give a job 10 seconds to clean up after SIGTERM before SIGKILL, instead of 2. Committed as fc0614d9 with "Fixes #1081". Build, the full cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and fmt --check all pass.

What changed:
• The value and its reason (src/commands_bg.rs): BG_STOP_GRACE is now 10s. Its comment no longer says "the same window BG_READER_GRACE gives". It now lists what other tools use for the same TERM-wait-KILL step (docker stop 10s, Kubernetes 30s, systemd 90s) and says why docker's number fits: it's the one where a person waits at a prompt. It also states the trade:
  • A job that exits on SIGTERM still stops in milliseconds.
  • Only a job that ignores SIGTERM or hangs on it makes /bg kill wait the full 10s. /quit waits one shared 10s however many jobs are running, and those jobs show exit -9 (SIGKILL) as before.
  • The user who lost work at 2s was the one whose cleanup on SIGTERM needed longer than that.
• A test seam instead of slow tests: I added kill_with(id, grace) and stop_running_with(grace). The real kill and stop_running call them with BG_STOP_GRACE, so this is a parameter on the existing code path, not a copy. The three tests that use jobs ignoring SIGTERM now pass a 1s grace and check timing against that, so the bg tests still finish in about 4s.
• Pinning test: bg_stop_grace_is_docker_stops_ten_seconds fails if the value changes and says to re-argue it first. I checked it: setting the value back to 2s makes it fail, and restoring 10s makes it pass.
• Docs: in docs/src/usage/commands.md, the /bg kill table row and the paragraph about kill and quit now say 10s. They also say that a job which exits on SIGTERM stops at once. I updated the matching comment in src/repl.rs too.

I left a few "2s grace" mentions alone (RUN_READER_GRACE in commands_run.rs, and the bash tool's tests in tools_group_kill_tests.rs). They're about how long output keeps being read after the shell exits, a separate setting this change doesn't touch.
